                  <td><code>000 (0x0)</code></td>
                  <td><code>imm[11:0] = 0x000</code></td>
                  <td><code>Trap: EnvironmentCall &rarr; mtvec</code></td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Valid (x0..x15)</span></td>
                  <td>Generates synchronous trap to Machine Mode monitor or OS runtime (system call). Saves return PC in <code>mepc</code> and sets <code>mcause</code> to 11.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr data-cat="system">
                  <td><code>ebreak</code></td>
                  <td><span class="fmt-badge fmt-i">I-Type</span></td>
                  <td><code>1110011 (0x73)</code></td>
                  <td><code>000 (0x0)</code></td>
                  <td><code>imm[11:0] = 0x001</code></td>
                  <td><code>Trap: Breakpoint &rarr; Debugger</code></td>
                  <td><span class="status-badge ok">✓ Valid (x0..x15)</span></td>
                  <td>Transfers execution control back to interactive hardware debugger (JTAG/OpenOCD) or breakpoint exception handler (sets <code>mcause = 3</code>).</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Sub-part B: Standard Modular Extensions -->
      <div class="isa-subtopic-block" style="margin-bottom: 1.75rem;">
        <h4 class="isa-subtopic-title">
          <span class="pill-badge badge-teal">MODULAR EXTENSIONS</span>
          Silicon Cost vs. Software Benefit of Standard Extensions
        </h4>
        <p class="isa-subtopic-text">
          Once the base integer ISA is chosen, hardware architects selectively attach modular extensions. Each extension demands an explicit silicon area budget to unlock specific software speedups:
        </p>

        <div class="isa-extensions-flow">
          <!-- Extension M -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-coral">EXTENSION 'M'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Integer Hardware Multiplication &amp; Division</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +3.0 &ndash; 6.0 kGE (or 1&ndash;4 DSP Slices)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Adds 8 instructions (<code>MUL</code>, <code>MULH</code>, <code>DIV</code>, <code>REM</code>, etc.). Hardware can range from a slow 34-cycle bit-serial shift-and-add circuit (PicoRV32), to an iterative Radix-4 divider (Ibex, SCR1), up to a fully-pipelined single-cycle multiplier (VeeR EL2).
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> <strong>15&times; to 35&times; acceleration</strong> over GCC software emulation routines (<code>__mulsi3</code>, <code>__divsi3</code>). Essential for digital filtering, motor control, and CoreMark benchmarking.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Omitted in pure housekeeping cores (SERV) to preserve zero-silicon footprint; mandatory for compute &amp; control microcontrollers (Hazard3, SCR1, VeeR).
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension C -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-lime">EXTENSION 'C'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Compressed 16-Bit Instructions (Code Density)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +600 &ndash; 1,200 Gates (Pre-decoder Logic)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Re-encodes the most common 32-bit instructions (loads, stores, branches, integer arithmetic) into 16-bit equivalents. Requires a small 16-to-32-bit instruction expander in the Fetch/Decode stage and unaligned instruction fetch handling.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>The Memory Die Area Paradox:</strong> Reduces compiled firmware size by <strong>25% &ndash; 35%</strong>. In a microcontroller SoC, on-chip SRAM/ROM typically consumes <strong>60% to 80% of total silicon die area</strong>. Spending ~800 gates of core logic to shrink SRAM from 64 KB to 48 KB drastically reduces total chip cost!
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Virtually universal in commercial production MCUs (Ibex, NEORV32, Hazard3, SCR1, VeeR EL2).
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension B -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-yellow">EXTENSION 'B'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Bit Manipulation (Zba, Zbb, Zbc, Zbs)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +1.5 &ndash; 3.0 kGE (ALU Additions)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Accelerates bitwise operations: address generation (<code>Zba</code>: <code>sh1add</code>, <code>sh2add</code>), bitfield operations (<code>Zbb</code>: <code>clz</code>, <code>ctz</code>, <code>cpop</code>, <code>rol</code>, <code>ror</code>), carryless multiply (<code>Zbc</code>: CRC acceleration), and single-bit set/clear/extract (<code>Zbs</code>: <code>bset</code>, <code>bclr</code>, <code>binv</code>, <code>bext</code>).
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> Replaces 5 to 15 instructions of masking, shifting, and branching with a single 1-cycle instruction. Slashes cryptographic hashing, peripheral register bit-flipping, and CRC calculation overhead.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Deployed in <strong>Hazard3</strong> (the RISC-V core in the Raspberry Pi RP2350 microcontroller) to deliver exceptional peripheral control and protocol processing.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension A -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-teal">EXTENSION 'A'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Atomic Memory Operations (AMO &amp; LR/SC)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +1.0 &ndash; 2.5 kGE + Bus Lock Hardware</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Provides Load-Reserved/Store-Conditional (<code>lr.w</code>/<code>sc.w</code>) and Atomic Read-Modify-Write instructions (<code>amoadd</code>, <code>amoswap</code>, etc.). Requires bus-level reservation monitors and exclusive bus transaction support.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> Enables lock-free multi-threading, inter-process communication, and mutex primitives in multi-core SoCs or SMP operating systems.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Unnecessary overhead in isolated single-core sensor controllers (PicoRV32, SERV); essential in Linux or multi-core environments (VexRiscv GenFull, NEORV32 multi-core).
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension Zfinx / F -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-purple">EXTENSION 'Zfinx'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Floating-Point in Integer Registers</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: FPU Logic Only (Zero Extra RF Area)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Standard <code>F</code> extension mandates 32 dedicated 32-bit floating-point registers (<code>f0 &ndash; f31</code>), doubling register file area and RTOS context-switch latency. <code>Zfinx</code> executes all IEEE-754 single-precision operations directly using the existing integer register file (<code>x0 &ndash; x31</code>).
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> Delivers true hardware IEEE-754 float acceleration for sensor fusion, PID motor loops, and audio DSP without paying the silicon penalty of a secondary register file.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Supported by <strong>NEORV32</strong> and <strong>VexRiscv</strong> for embedded DSP applications where area is constrained.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Privileged Architecture & PMP -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-ink">PRIVILEGED &amp; PMP</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Privileged Modes (M/U) &amp; Physical Memory Protection</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +1.5 &ndash; 5.0 kGE (Comparators &amp; CSRs)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Separates Machine Mode (<code>M</code>, baremetal supervisor) from User Mode (<code>U</code>, unprivileged app). Physical Memory Protection (PMP) implements programmable address checking registers (typically 4, 8, or 16 regions) that enforce Read/Write/Execute rules on every memory access.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Hardware Security Payoff:</strong> Prevents buffer overflows or malicious code from accessing private cryptographic keys or critical peripheral control registers. Enables hardware roots-of-trust and isolated enclaves.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Central to <strong>Ibex</strong> in Google's OpenTitan silicon root-of-trust, combined with Dual-Core Lockstep (DCLS) for fault detection.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Sub-part C: Cross-Core Architectural Decision Matrix -->
      <div class="isa-subtopic-block">
        <h4 class="isa-subtopic-title">
          <span class="pill-badge badge-yellow">ARCHITECTURAL DECISION MATRIX</span>
          Why Evaluated Cores Selected Their Specific ISA Configurations
        </h4>
        <p class="isa-subtopic-text">
          The table below correlates the ISA configuration of each evaluated open-source core with its underlying design rationale and target silicon mission:
