        <!-- 2.4.2 SUPERSCALAR HAZARD & INSTRUCTION PAIRING MATRIX -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-paper">PAIRING MATRIX</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">VeeR EL2 Dual-Issue Instruction Pairing Rules</h4>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            In an in-order superscalar pipeline, achieving dual-issue execution depends on strict architectural pairing constraints. When two candidate instructions meet the compatibility criteria, they dispatch in parallel; otherwise, the second instruction stalls for 1 cycle:
          </p>

          <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <table class="data-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Instruction Pair Combination</th>
                  <th>Dual-Issue Capable?</th>
                  <th>Cycle Latency</th>
                  <th>Hardware Mechanism &amp; Constraint</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>ALU + ALU (Independent)</strong><br /><code>add x1, x2, x3</code> &amp; <code>sub x4, x5, x6</code></td>
                  <td><span class="score-badge" style="background: #b7df69; color: #fffaf0;">YES</span></td>
                  <td>1 clock cycle (CPI = 0.5)</td>
                  <td>Dispatches Inst 0 to ALU 1 and Inst 1 to ALU 2 concurrently. Both write back to separate ports on cycle edge.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>ALU + Load (Independent)</strong><br /><code>addi x1, x2, 4</code> &amp; <code>lw x3, 0(x4)</code></td>
                  <td><span class="score-badge" style="background: #b7df69; color: #fffaf0;">YES</span></td>
                  <td>1 clock cycle</td>
                  <td>Inst 0 uses ALU 1; Inst 1 computes memory address in ALU 2 and asserts DCCM load request.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Branch + ALU (Independent)</strong><br /><code>bne x1, x2, target</code> &amp; <code>add x3, x4, x5</code></td>
                  <td><span class="score-badge" style="background: #b7df69; color: #fffaf0;">YES</span></td>
                  <td>1 clock cycle</td>
                  <td>Branch evaluates in dedicated Branch Unit; ALU executes concurrently. If branch taken, ALU result commits or rolls back based on branch order.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>RAW Data Hazard (Dependent ALU)</strong><br /><code>add x1, x2, x3</code> &amp; <code>sub x4, x1, x5</code></td>
                  <td><span class="badge badge-coral">NO (Stall 1 cyc)</span></td>
                  <td>2 clock cycles</td>
                  <td>Inst 1 consumes <code>x1</code> produced by Inst 0. Inter-instruction bypass forwards result from EX1 to EX2, but dual-issue is split.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Load-to-Use Hazard</strong><br /><code>lw x1, 0(x2)</code> &amp; <code>add x3, x1, x4</code></td>
                  <td><span class="badge badge-coral">NO (Stall 2 cyc)</span></td>
                  <td>3 clock cycles</td>
                  <td>Memory word requires 1 cycle DCCM read latency; consuming instruction stalls in dispatch stage until data valid.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Multiplier + Multiplier</strong><br /><code>mul x1, x2, x3</code> &amp; <code>mul x4, x5, x6</code></td>
                  <td><span class="badge badge-coral">NO (Structural Stall)</span></td>
                  <td>2 clock cycles</td>
                  <td>Only one hardware multiplier pipeline is provisioned to conserve silicon area. Second multiply serialized.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <!-- Architectural Decision Guide -->
          <div class="arch-verdict-box" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
            <h5 class="verdict-title">&#9878; System Architect's Decision Guide: When to Select Archetype 4?</h5>
            <ul class="concept-list">
              <li><strong>Select VeeR EL2 when:</strong> You are architecting enterprise-grade storage controllers (NVMe, SAS, SATA), high-throughput network interfaces (SmartNICs), or industrial real-time compute complexes where single-issue CPI &ge; 1.0 is an intolerable throughput bottleneck.</li>
              <li><strong>Select VeeR EL2 when:</strong> You require the highest possible DMIPS/MHz (1.68) and CoreMark/MHz (2.56) in open silicon, backed by Western Digital's silicon qualification and mass-production testing.</li>
              <li><strong>Avoid Archetype 4 when:</strong> Your gate count budget is below 40 kGE or you cannot provision a 4R/2W multiported register file and dual-ported 64-bit SRAMs.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
